ed by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"35d59a84-f575-4181-a16c-a3d49d3f1be7","sessionId":"8c71d1bf-4999-4d90-89f1-568a695f4e16","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"e5d23ddd-20c1-4cab-b29a-3278d43873c1","sessionId":"f8dbc1df-2227-441c-b598-fa396cbdf4e4","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"3e71a3db-bcc1-4d62-a1f2-fded0e648e78","sessionId":"c6517f3d-3959-4bd3-af1a-a2b2f78a734c","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"ac7b2ff3-59b3-447c-a830-73c5e5662dd1","sessionId":"604f2d19-583a-4af3-bf26-0750b19d38db","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"9eafa9c1-8aa2-4532-997b-3fe1eb5eb375","sessionId":"fdeaa2c4-ba53-46e4-aef5-b33350eeca50","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"5b94bf06-b18f-45c5-8438-6af7b7ee1ee3","sessionId":"81050da2-e517-4d4d-9c7d-5daf76537de5","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"ed8807da-e833-49f7-b732-72de240bf2d1","sessionId":"4257d725-a6e9-4550-9dda-1d5749097f23","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"c2cc3392-9c7b-4fb9-ae12-ea4ce30126a3","sessionId":"3a18832e-348a-4ebc-b638-4c54592fbea1","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"b1f52ce9-cd0e-45fe-9d72-cce7380bf8a9","sessionId":"1d429cb0-ae87-4e90-97d6-edb8a953ce47","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"3b3d5379-2ac8-4ebc-8391-1d2a7793ddc5","sessionId":"5ba8b1d0-ffd9-44f8-bc14-16028edf80a0","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"9ae83481-bee2-45ce-a188-7c5b94e840e5","sessionId":"c365f800-42a2-4620-b856-237b21b9f96b","text":"Anthropic input token caching unsupported by coyote","timestamp":1779901329900},{"commentId":"302871d8-8b6d-48ab-ab5d-55c571526b5d","sessionId":"5d09ab7f-2937-4592-82cf-a84ab8057145","text":"Tool calls wasted due to poor understanding of the coyote session spec. I think this is a good candidate for a new skill","timestamp":1779901630822},{"commentId":"f06dfacb-8a6c-4d3e-849e-6440e9067b51","sessionId":"26f6c549-b2a3-45f0-95e3-4b2c48f2f8bf","text":"Not using sonnet-4.6","timestamp":1780141205705},{"commentId":"fe524c98-db80-4dda-af93-a17f5baf3a6a","sessionId":"e68d6a71-0b01-4d27-816f-3aeb28975492","text":"Not using sonnet-4.6","timestamp":1780141205705},{"commentId":"80356b3f-5c8c-4341-9ebe-a4c462560cab","sessionId":"a4719a84-0512-477b-aa63-e32975914865","text":"Not using sonnet-4.6","timestamp":1780141205705},{"commentId":"93b91216-5897-44fd-acee-8f3fba9eb4d5","sessionId":"5e70131c-2cd8-4a9c-b0a7-22f9e008ecb1","text":"Repeated `hed` match failures. Updated `hed-tools` skill to avoid failure modes.","timestamp":1780211259131},{"commentId":"9a8e4199-caac-4042-aac8-039c436f7146","sessionId":"8ce8377e-034d-4919-a466-d19177484648","text":"`-Werror` causes the compilation to stop immediately after the first warning, resulting in multiple failed compilations to fix all warnings. The agent should instead compile without `-Werror` to catch all warnings and then fix them in one go. Created a `c-programming` skill to capture these learnings.","timestamp":1780212052564},{"commentId":"e69d023d-b4f1-49d8-b2e4-cdf2f9ad79ed","sessionId":"80a106bd-0c36-4d52-baea-27fa25e7ec96","text":"I think this consecutive tool diversity LCL OOC is an artifact of a mean shift due to transitioning from the pi ","timestamp":1780213405254},{"commentId":"b1c463f5-27c1-4c57-b41a-89705bd22624","sessionId":"08abd889-7e88-4599-982c-f75c5f984386","text":"I think this consecutive tool diversity LCL OOC is an artifact of a mean shift due to transitioning from the pi ","timestamp":1780213405254},{"commentId":"81361e08-a412-4aaf-8e24-986a68c748b4","sessionId":"29937aef-c459-4859-84a3-40fae762754f","text":"I think this consecutive tool diversity LCL OOC is an artifact of a mean shift due to transitioning from the pi ","timestamp":1780213405254},{"commentId":"cda45e9f-567e-46fe-9deb-7efa70268692","sessionId":"1cff648c-e447-4bec-898f-8828f99ab7ed","text":"Assistant message heavy ideation conversation. So there aren't many tool calls.","timestamp":1780215013680},{"commentId":"51bba863-0a86-4e6f-9bee-9ddaa8aa20e8","sessionId":"7a0fab94-fbc7-46cd-ba44-cbcec14bafc9","text":"High tool call similarity due to inline throwaway python scripts with boilerplate being rewritten over and over again. Added a throwaway scripts skill to make using throwaway scripts more efficient.","timestamp":1780224863688},{"commentId":"c6ecdc48-2589-4ca9-9ea8-5c34f9e8c4ab","sessionId":"9fcdb258-94ec-4565-b4f5-ab54b8377d5f","text":"High tool call similarity due to inline throwaway python scripts with boilerplate being rewritten over and over again. Added a throwaway scripts skill to make using throwaway scripts more efficient.","timestamp":1780224863688},{"commentId":"03402af8-1729-49b8-891f-7d025df479d0","sessionId":"30a9ae83-93f9-4c9a-8286-d4d5f8f2e972","text":"High tool call similarity due to inline throwaway python scripts with boilerplate being rewritten over and over again. Added a throwaway scripts skill to make using throwaway scripts more efficient.","timestamp":1780224863688},{"commentId":"69c4a2c2-506d-4f12-b0ab-f7b95695d3e8","sessionId":"859b978f-f3da-40b2-9955-fed5a8c17196","text":"Tool call similarity variance is high due to one pair of highly similar inline python script tool calls, while the other tool call pairs are fairly diverse.","timestamp":1780225322328},{"commentId":"8d17a3e1-a6a9-44ac-982b-b67a25d3381c","sessionId":"39048cde-b0b6-486d-8d2b-16d4671bc7b7","text":"Pair of identical large printf tool calls piped to a file. This resulted in a high degree of similarity for those tool calls, even though the rest of the tool calls were reasonably diverse. This resulted in a high tool call similarity variance.","timestamp":1780225702741},{"commentId":"93a043f8-5872-4d57-9a2a-d0ebc5030a27","sessionId":"39048cde-b0b6-486d-8d2b-16d4671bc7b7","text":"The printfs were not exactly identical. The agent was trying to resolve a quoting issue and just regenerated everything.","timestamp":1780226014985},{"commentId":"1b60499c-1663-4a5d-a19b-46ad99116a4c","sessionId":"41693701-13c3-4de8-9c21-17744599cc44","text":"failed to create a file before opening it in `hed` resulting in the stdin text being wasted. This had to be rewritten in the following command resulting in a high degree of tool call similarity for this particular tool call turn.","timestamp":1780226847697},{"commentId":"8cbfac54-d0f7-4ad5-aee7-eba78827b751","sessionId":"41693701-13c3-4de8-9c21-17744599cc44","text":"Updated hed-tools skill with learnings to try and prevent these errors in the future.","timestamp":1780227530444},{"commentId":"aeff8e6f-3735-42cc-9332-dd872f0da2ae","sessionId":"5f2fe69b-75a6-48aa-a593-dc8170f58df6","text":"Repeated throwaway python boilerplate was written instead of a temp script on disk. This inflated the consecutive tool call similarity for these tool calls.","timestamp":1780227702297},{"commentId":"c62e6153-f93a-46bc-b924-5f274ebde97e","sessionId":"8934341b-f70a-499e-a010-447d5f6cb990","text":"`hed` and search related command repetition. Modified `hed-tools` to prevent these issues.","timestamp":1780229718694},{"commentId":"6107048f-37f0-4580-a49f-db337c0be7ef","sessionId":"dc354e2b-55a2-41fb-a13a-a8d64b76e5b2","text":"This session created the `throwaway-scripts` skill, intended to resolve the very sort of tool call duplication observed in the session.","timestamp":1780232452024},{"commentId":"ba19bdca-ebfb-465b-994a-e60ca42a5f05","sessionId":"62a2976d-05ee-432c-8f09-581a258f2af4","text":"`throwaway-scripts` was not loaded before writing the throwaway python scripts, resulting in nearly duplicate inline script tool calls.","timestamp":1780232765189},{"commentId":"86859154-7430-4eb7-9281-16179f76ee2b","sessionId":"14320e14-395d-4667-9cab-c07b1d75f08d","text":"`throwaway-scripts` skill was not loaded, resulting in a large number of similar inline jq scripts being written and executed sequentially. ","timestamp":1780233037990},{"commentId":"663b7aea-e398-4461-8ad3-9b2d37a93218","sessionId":"e44d2be5-2739-4e66-be1e-de99168b1315","text":"`throwaway-scripts` skill not loaded, resulting in a lot of inline jq scripts being rewritten for each invocation.","timestamp":1780233327399},{"commentId":"81893d3e-0597-4115-87a6-c1ed901ad7f2","sessionId":"c50fb9d6-747f-4060-f3cd-2ebc38cfd02d","text":"Accidentally switched models to `gpt-5-mini` resulting in a massive cache invalidation.","timestamp":1780236598170},{"commentId":"2575ab61-89e6-4c00-afbc-d1ef1f62509c","sessionId":"6fee652f-9e72-4c7c-ad9d-ce7143015e16","text":"Seems to be a normal session outlier","timestamp":1780237140947},{"commentId":"53b13066-6264-4b72-8a5c-f2173993b126","sessionId":"421e9c91-48de-4123-bc98-d50839ab4598","text":"Inspection of the session history protocol suggests that an OpenAI model was used, rather than the typical Sonnet 3.6","timestamp":1780237517888},{"commentId":"b56c5fb7-5bf5-4039-9f88-4d455203f5bd","sessionId":"e563dfaf-5285-4817-b17e-7d4e3e0a9ed7","text":"`throwaway-scripts` skill still isn't being loaded. Updated the skill to hopefully allow the agent to load it more reliably.","timestamp":1780237878132},{"commentId":"d2a6b596-276d-4057-9070-3ffe50b20ecb","sessionId":"6c6cb64d-f5fe-4fec-81d1-7cce8cffd8d3","text":"The session's work naturally split into phases \u2014 a repetitive PDF-scrolling phase (many nearly-identical `pdftotext \u2026 | sed -n 'N,Mp'` calls) and a repetitive file-writing phase (three nearly-identical `cat > skill-file` calls) \u2014 interspersed with sharp transitions between completely unlike commands at phase boundaries. This bimodal mix of very-high-similarity and very-low-similarity consecutive pairs produced an unusually large within-session standard deviation of JSD S values, pushing the s-chart above the control limit.","timestamp":1780331705758},{"commentId":"cafee060-5445-400a-8355-a649e9427c4f","sessionId":"4fc2ed5d-36c7-42ba-9ae6-560096da9ec1","text":"The below-LCL signal is driven primarily by the 79 **recon\u2192recon** transitions (55% of all pairs, mean S = 13.1), where the agent jumped between entirely different files and grep patterns in rapid succession, producing near-maximum JSD divergence on the `command` argument each time. The read\u2194edit transitions (46 pairs, mean S \u2248 10.8\u201311.1) compounded this further, as each `hgrep`/`sed` lookup was followed by a `hed` invocation on a structurally unrelated path \u2014 the only transitions that pulled the mean upward were the 6 `write \u2192 write/edit` pairs (mean S = 134.8), where a temp-file `cat >` was immediately followed by a `hed` edit of related content, but these were too few to rescue the overall mean.","timestamp":1780332405018},{"commentId":"ff50020b-effe-4ad3-a072-edbd8e856092","sessionId":"d8a29753-973f-4693-a6d3-925e77335a5e","text":"The agent loaded the `throwaway-scripts` skill but failed to apply it at the critical decision point: after receiving partial results from call 9's Python script, it iterated with a near-identical revised version (call 10) rather than reasoning from the existing output. By that point the skill content was far back in the context window, reducing its salience precisely when it was most needed.","timestamp":1780333310370},{"commentId":"fdf2adf0-4a4c-4c7c-ae25-0125aea044e9","sessionId":"c112fa8a-bc1f-44bb-8a45-9752dc3bb134","text":"The session's consecutive tool-call pairs fell into two structurally distinct phases \u2014 three small `cat SKILL.md` calls followed by progressively larger analysis commands \u2014 creating a wide spread of S_k values within the session. The final pair's S_k was especially inflated because both calls shared the long session file path as a high-frequency token, and S_k scales with token count, driving the within-session standard deviation out of control.","timestamp":1780334162105},{"commentId":"052de6ef-4687-41dd-9cee-f15a5acaabb3","sessionId":"994c1fb5-84ca-4d83-95bf-4f62ac7986ac","text":"The agent produced two consecutive large Python scripts on the same domain because it changed its mind mid-approach \u2014 it wrote a comprehensive script to `/tmp/` and then, rather than running what it had written, abandoned it and ran a simpler inline version instead. That write-and-discard followed by a do-over is a one-cycle loop: the agent failed to commit to a plan before executing it, spent one tool call on work it immediately threw away, and then repeated the intent with a different implementation. The s-chart signal, while driven by the N_k scaling mechanism, was pointing at a real inefficiency \u2014 just a very compact one.","timestamp":1780334739918},{"commentId":"96ea06bb-c465-4c2e-87dc-30fb1f96faf7","sessionId":"4f9a0ca0-7993-454c-94d3-6cff0fa177c4","text":"The agent ran the same Python analysis script twice against the same session file without fully processing the first result, creating a small repetitive loop. This, combined with the extreme contrast between large script-writing commands and short inspection commands, inflated the within-session S_k standard deviation above the s-chart UCL.","timestamp":1780335041042},{"commentId":"987e28a2-d591-474a-85e0-715034ce6294","sessionId":"994c1fb5-84ca-4d83-95bf-4f62ac7986ac","text":"Updated the `throwaway-scripts` skill to avoid these tiny loops.","timestamp":1780335071901},{"commentId":"dcdcb3a1-cef5-4c36-877a-ea2e5dc8337e","sessionId":"5b206812-6e52-41d9-bfac-4cccf52cab53","text":"The session was a documentation reorganisation task that required writing multiple large, structurally similar MIL-STD-498 documents via `hed -M` with multi-kilobyte `stdin` payloads \u2014 these produced enormous S_k values (up to ~2,963) because the documents share heavy boilerplate vocabulary. Those giant S_k spikes sat alongside many short, diverse read/inspect shell commands with S_k values of 3\u201322, and it is that extreme within-session spread between the two populations that drove the s-chart above its control limit.","timestamp":1780338642026},{"commentId":"81a7e9be-3904-4523-901d-8519f316ee2f","sessionId":"c032154e-3368-4d6f-a2b5-6a42d2eefc2d","text":"The session wrote a large Python analysis script (~300 lines) in a single shell call, producing a high-token pair with substantial vocabulary overlap against the follow-on calls that drove several S_k values far above the session's baseline. The wide spread between those large-N pairs and the small-N pairs (short `cat` and `find` calls) inflated the within-session S_k standard deviation beyond the UCL.","timestamp":1780339750210},{"commentId":"88baaa1c-998b-4f23-a07c-2206109933bb","sessionId":"bc27b4e2-03bc-44e6-bd08-f1d502b49fe3","text":"Two large overlapping python scripts written in a row creating large similarity variance","timestamp":1780339891946},{"commentId":"d8920ded-ded0-4a2f-ae10-022de5a4b88a","sessionId":"faf6519a-9691-4505-82a2-0d927e849641","text":"The root cause of the high tool call failure rate was that the agent performed a lot of exploratory shell/tool calls (browser automation, file edits, searches) without ensuring the local environment, correct skill paths, or the project's required invocation conventions. Missing dependencies (Playwright/Firefox), a wrong skill-file path, inline-perl quoting and malformed hed scripts, plus stale grep targets produced multiple independent shell failures that raised the overall error rate.\n","timestamp":1780341371741},{"commentId":"f86811ef-6837-4fa6-9d0d-a8424690d76c","sessionId":"2be2f85e-6c24-46c9-a9e6-da84898d3263","text":"The agent captured `@hash` addresses from `hgrep`, performed intervening writes that changed the file, then attempted to reuse those stale hashes in subsequent `hed` invocations \u2014 causing `no match for hash address` failures. It also composed `hed` scripts using `sed`-style commands (`y///`, hold-space ops, etc.) that are not part of `ed`'s command set, producing `unknown command` errors.\n\nTwo new Pitfalls entries were added to the `hed-tools` skill: one that explicitly forbids carrying `@hash` values across write boundaries and mandates a fresh `hgrep -K` before every new `hed` invocation, and one that names the specific `sed`/`ex`/`vi` commands most likely to be mistakenly used in `hed` scripts and directs agents to cross-check against the quick-reference table before issuing any multi-command script.","timestamp":1780341933901},{"commentId":"3765f869-a60f-4fad-86a6-38911349bb34","sessionId":"4fc2ed5d-36c7-42ba-9ae6-560096da9ec1","text":"**Root cause:** Session `4fc2ed5d` had a disproportionate tool failure rate because the agent reused `@hash` addresses captured before intervening file writes, causing `hed` to fail with \"no match for hash address,\" and because build-verification and reconnaissance commands were structured as `grep` pipelines whose exit-1-on-no-match was recorded as a tool error even when no match was the correct outcome. These two patterns accounted for all 9 failures (5 stale-hash, 4 benign grep exits).\n\n**Action taken:** The `hed-tools` skill's Mandatory Pre-Edit Checklist was strengthened to explicitly state that `@hash` addresses \u2014 not just text patterns \u2014 expire at every file write and must be re-acquired with `hgrep -K` before the next edit. A new pitfall entry was added covering the two canonical grep false-positive scenarios (reconnaissance probes and build-output filtering), with concrete alternatives that decouple the informational result from the exit code.","timestamp":1780342369823},{"commentId":"8bb749a5-b47a-45b9-a297-9b76cb45cab7","sessionId":"5b206812-6e52-41d9-bfac-4cccf52cab53","text":"The session's three genuine `hed` failures shared a single root cause: the agent reused a stale `@hash` address after the file had been modified, then compounded the error by falling back to a hardcoded line number that was out of range, and separately used an `(expr)` computed-address prefix (`(.-1)r`) that `hed` does not support. The remaining two failures were false positives \u2014 a trailing `ls todo/` and a no-match `grep` both returned non-zero exit codes for benign reasons.\n\nThe `hed-tools` skill was updated with two targeted additions: a recovery action appended to the stale-hash pitfall directing the agent to re-run `hgrep -K` rather than fall back to line numbers when `invalid address` fires, and a new pitfall entry explicitly naming `(expr)` address expressions as invalid with a WRONG/RIGHT code example pointing to `@hash+n`/`@hash-n` as the correct alternative.","timestamp":1780342927537},{"commentId":"9da6c6fa-c67c-4ce9-be8a-c71c22c8ddea","sessionId":"8556fc33-62fb-49c8-bc6c-aadead25230a","text":"Accidental model switch resulted in cache invalidation, causing a spike in uncached input tokens","timestamp":1780343099750},{"commentId":"0e8e7e91-fc17-4fea-bceb-f7d71102cb3c","sessionId":"67054edb-8daf-4970-8c46-b1dbccb9229d","text":"The subagent edited `plan/project-plan.md` across multiple user-feedback turns, reusing `@hash` addresses and text patterns captured before earlier writes had already changed the file. By the time those stale values were used in subsequent `hed` calls, the underlying content had moved or been removed, causing `no match for hash address` and `hgrep` exit-1 failures.\n\nThe `hed-tools` skill was updated with a new pitfall entry \u2014 **Iterative user-feedback sessions: accumulated file-state staleness** \u2014 prescribing a full file-state reset at the start of every user-feedback turn. Checklist item 1 was also updated to cross-reference the new pitfall alongside the existing hash-staleness entry.\n","timestamp":1780378745942},{"commentId":"90f42593-edf8-434d-951e-cfc6354a3da9","sessionId":"283f40fb-c6b9-40c5-a179-4a46b4a88e36","text":"Subagent test","timestamp":1780502550262},{"commentId":"4755f40a-80a2-460c-945f-19010f106ac9","sessionId":"a2066aaa-4e41-4e2b-8e90-8bdbc6950b51","text":"subagent test","timestamp":1780502601921},{"commentId":"ca04e02a-e5c4-4180-99fb-75f965255f5c","sessionId":"0cd8ea7d-babf-4522-992b-00dbad23d581","text":"Switched to medium thinking for implementation","timestamp":1780749576143},{"commentId":"11b8ef96-a483-4014-8efe-7b2544e92b09","sessionId":"e6ddd7f4-ac82-43e9-91d6-41cb78a2f49d","text":"Switched to medium thinking for implementation","timestamp":1780749595235},{"commentId":"1328ca99-c1d7-443b-9886-080597a9bf8a","sessionId":"8e7f2a27-86fa-4fb3-a6ac-3665ca8c5359","text":"Switched to medium thinking for implementation","timestamp":1780749663220},{"commentId":"9c87ddc6-a405-4bb7-ae57-18730b01907c","sessionId":"43652b66-e8bf-45c3-b948-f91606e3c389","text":"The failures all share a single root cause: the model placed the `=N` exact-count assertion between the `s` command letter and the opening delimiter, writing `s=1/old/new/` instead of `s/old/new/=1`. Since **hed** interprets the character immediately following `s` as the delimiter, `=` becomes the delimiter and the search pattern becomes the literal text `1/old/new/` \u2014 a string that never matches any line in the file. The rest of the script is consumed as a continuation of that unterminated search pattern, producing `unexpected end-of-file` and cascading `?` tokens for every subsequent command.\n\nThe fix was two-fold. In the code, every compiled pattern now stores a copy of its pattern string in a new `pat_str` field, and all three \"no match\" error sites include that pattern (or hash value) in the diagnostic message \u2014 so `script, line 4: no match for pattern \"1/old/new/\"` makes the syntax mistake instantly visible. In the hed-tools skill, a new anti-pattern section documents the correct placement of `=N` as a trailing suffix, shows side-by-side wrong and right examples, and explains exactly why the wrong form is syntactically valid but silently wrong.","timestamp":1781337677536},{"commentId":"8963cec6-c77a-4b51-92f6-2e41e9223105","sessionId":"4ee915e2-f0b1-4125-9017-ff8d260de533","text":"Created multiple essentially identical sessions because I though the LLM was unresponsive after aborting an agentic loop. It turned out that it was just thinking for a long time and this isn't flushed until the thinking is completed.","timestamp":1781436258814},{"commentId":"0f93e4de-8605-4d72-914c-49baa1f00cb5","sessionId":"7d4121d6-7d30-41e8-5876-db2feef8226f","text":"Created multiple essentially identical sessions because I though the LLM was unresponsive after aborting an agentic loop. It turned out that it was just thinking for a long time and this isn't flushed until the thinking is completed.","timestamp":1781436258814},{"commentId":"f2a618dd-7f85-4b09-8f1e-4de90983e235","sessionId":"076b1ccd-1c94-4146-baaf-df5b5d4ce599","text":"Created multiple essentially identical sessions because I though the LLM was unresponsive after aborting an agentic loop. It turned out that it was just thinking for a long time and this isn't flushed until the thinking is completed.","timestamp":1781436258814},{"commentId":"73f9b019-719b-4bdf-95a6-05c65a602c05","sessionId":"12d4c39a-a6dc-463d-b293-f340ada7a84e","text":"Created multiple essentially identical sessions because I though the LLM was unresponsive after aborting an agentic loop. It turned out that it was just thinking for a long time and this isn't flushed until the thinking is completed.","timestamp":1781436258814},{"commentId":"9419a1e9-f089-4109-baec-c252d785ded2","sessionId":"8003253a-c469-47e8-9b02-fd23e4dfe571","text":"Created multiple essentially identical sessions because I though the LLM was unresponsive after aborting an agentic loop. It turned out that it was just thinking for a long time and this isn't flushed until the thinking is completed.","timestamp":1781436258814}],"logYMode":true,"modelFilter":[],"name":"Coyote Dev","setupSessionIds":["dc354e2b-55a2-41fb-a13a-a8d64b76e5b2","a847f72e-7e04-4982-8eeb-3b97dc930761","d3d7ed3a-9086-4fd7-9b1f-caa4281429ed","626a019b-ad4b-4a33-9e25-31617087ed58","e563dfaf-5285-4817-b17e-7d4e3e0a9ed7","c8338910-9105-42b8-b444-5c234feca29b","9158b3e2-9b52-4f14-8314-df075befd1ab","3a89b514-ea30-4a0a-9886-52bc9400cd67","8e5d063a-1d43-4a9b-8b74-9a412ce4a1c1","14320e14-395d-4667-9cab-c07b1d75f08d","ab42bca7-a7e2-4f87-9908-91a7ebde6f05","2fb2d300-e808-4aff-bcdd-46ac4108c8ee","30a9ae83-93f9-4c9a-8286-d4d5f8f2e972","2cb486e5-c978-45cd-aa71-a16846e48d30","2633f332-c820-4510-b6b8-81454b3167f4","6a092141-238b-4e0f-892a-c9fc6ed97a22","6f925e39-f952-463f-b156-5d8bfa0c5706","d6cadc02-c9b5-45a4-a386-da3be6bcd17a","b8148ede-e9f2-4cb5-a145-ff104b7bc591","dfdad1ac-2e96-45e8-80d7-79142e9e0dbe","e44d2be5-2739-4e66-be1e-de99168b1315","39048cde-b0b6-486d-8d2b-16d4671bc7b7","80b0a4c5-4276-4d4c-b69b-e077f0dd5838","342b2720-23f5-4941-a53b-0b9d2fd34bdd","859b978f-f3da-40b2-9955-fed5a8c17196","62a2976d-05ee-432c-8f09-581a258f2af4","5d99972f-ba70-4657-b8ec-cd7fa97ffef2","f2150d73-94d5-4453-b0c9-46725f0d707e"],"sourceDirectories":["/home/gtnoble/Projects/coyote","/home/gtnoble/Projects/oed","/home/gtnoble/Projects/grep","/home/gtnoble/Projects/pi_acme","/home/gtnoble/Projects/pi-acme","/home/gtnoble/Projects/digiplot"],"version":10,"workspaceId":""}
